[size=5][b]b-bridge: out-of-process Direct3D 9 rendering for GTA IV: The Complete Edition[/b][/size]
[i]A research release. Fork of NVIDIA's bridge-remix. Source, issues and releases: [url=https://github.com/gutbash/b-bridge]github.com/gutbash/b-bridge[/url][/i]

[size=4][b]Abstract[/b][/size]
GTA IV is a 32-bit process. Every resource the graphics driver allocates on the game's behalf (textures, shadow maps, render targets, staging buffers) is mapped into the same 4 GB address space as the game's own data, and a heavily modded install exhausts that space long before it exhausts RAM or VRAM. b-bridge moves the entire Direct3D 9 device into a separate 64-bit process. The game keeps a thin 32-bit d3d9.dll that forwards every call over a shared-memory channel to a 64-bit server, which renders through DXVK on Vulkan. On the test system this returned about 880 MB of address space to the game and held frame time at parity with in-process DXVK, allowing a 5 GB texture-pack stack to run where the same install previously crashed within minutes.

[size=4][b]Problem[/b][/size]
The address-space wall, not memory, is the practical ceiling on GTA IV modding. Large-address-aware flags, streaming-budget tuning, the -nomemrestrict family and texture-memory caps in DXVK reduce pressure but cannot change the fact that driver allocations and game allocations share one 32-bit space. Measured on the reference install before this work: largest contiguous free region as low as 5 MB with view distance at 100, and a streaming-allocator crash band at 2.8 to 3.0 GB committed.

[size=4][b]Method[/b][/size]
The IPC layer of NVIDIA RTX Remix already marshals D3D9 across processes; its purpose there is to feed a path tracer. b-bridge keeps the transport and discards the renderer: the server loads an unmodified DXVK 3.0.2 instead of the Remix runtime, and the client is tuned for a game that issues 15,000 to 70,000 D3D9 calls per frame.

Changes against upstream:
[list]
[*]Client frame pacer (waitable timer plus spin), so pacing happens in the game process rather than the server. bridge.conf: clientFrameCap
[*]State-block transfer plans: precomputed dirty index lists for the D3DSBT_ALL blocks GTA IV applies about 475 times per frame
[*]StateBatch: render, sampler and stage state, textures, shaders, streams and shader constants packed into one record per flush. bridge.conf: clientStateBatch
[*]Batched command index publish. bridge.conf: clientCmdPublishBatch
[*]Merged device/channel lock; import-free spinlock, so ASI loaders that hook kernel32 cannot re-enter it
[*]Per-window wait statistics in bridge32.log; assert logger no longer dereferences a dead channel
[*]Server: StateBatch replay; vanilla-DXVK mode as the supported configuration; responses only for calls that return data
[/list]

[size=4][b]Results[/b][/size]
Reference system: Intel i7-9700K, NVIDIA GeForce RTX 2070 (8 GB), driver 610.74, 2560x1440 borderless, GTA IV CE 1.2.0.59 with FusionFix. Frame times are means over a fixed 60-second scripted drive, uncapped. Memory figures are from an in-process address-space monitor at view and detail distance 100.

[list]
[*]In-process DXVK, same route (6 runs): 16.9 ms. Largest free region at view 100: 5 MB
[*]b-bridge, upstream client (5 runs): 22.4 ms. About 800 MB
[*]b-bridge, transfer plans + responses off (4 runs): 18.6 ms. About 800 MB
[*]b-bridge, final client (5 runs): 13.6 ms. About 800 MB
[*]b-bridge, final client, 5 GB texture-pack stack: 16.4 ms. 735 to 819 MB
[/list]
The batching work is what brings the bridged frame time below the in-process figure; the transport itself costs time, and the client hides it by sending fewer, larger messages. The five figures in the Images tab show the per-stage frame time, the frame-time distribution, the address-space comparison, the cost of a content and post-processing stack, and GPU utilisation through a session.

[size=4][b]Limitations[/b][/size]
These are the boundaries of what was tested. Nothing outside them should be assumed to work.
[list]
[*][b]NVIDIA only.[/b] Every measurement was taken on one RTX 2070 with one driver version. No AMD or Intel GPU has run this build. DXVK itself is vendor-neutral, but the bridge server's device creation and swapchain path have not been exercised on any other Vulkan driver.
[*][b]One machine, one configuration.[/b] One CPU, one resolution and refresh rate, borderless windowed only. Exclusive fullscreen is disabled by the shipped dxvk.conf because the mode switch loses the device across the process boundary.
[*][b]One game build and one mod stack.[/b] GTA IV CE 1.2.0.59 with FusionFix loaded through an ASI loader. Other patch levels (1.0.7.0, 1.0.8.0), the unpatched Complete Edition, and FusionFix loaded through its own d3d9.dll are untested.
[*][b]Raster only.[/b] GTA IV is a deferred renderer and the Remix path-traced pipeline does not produce a usable image with it. That pipeline is not shipped.
[*][b]CPU-bound scenes do not improve.[/b] Every D3D9 call still crosses the process boundary. The gain is address space; frame time is held at parity, not reduced, in scenes limited by the game's render thread.
[*][b]Startup desync, about one launch in ten.[/b] The server process never comes up, its log is not written, and the client exits after 12 seconds. Relaunching succeeds. Not yet isolated.
[*][b]First device creation fails on every launch.[/b] bridge64.log shows one failed CreateDevice (0x8876086c) before the real device is created. This is the game probing with a zero refresh rate and is harmless.
[*][b]Overlays on the server side get no input.[/b] A ReShade Vulkan layer in the server renders correctly but its overlay and hotkeys are dead, because the window belongs to the game process. Such tools must be configured by file.
[*][b]DXVK 3.0.2 only.[/b] Newer DXVK releases have not been validated with this server.
[*][b]Session length.[/b] The longest single measured session is about 25 minutes. Multi-hour stability has not been characterised.
[/list]

[size=4][b]Installation[/b][/size]
[list=1]
[*]If your GTAIV\d3d9.dll is FusionFix's wrapper (about 175 KB), rename it to d3d9Hooked.dll and make sure dinput8.dll (Ultimate ASI Loader) is present so plugins\GTAIV.EFLC.FusionFix.asi keeps loading. If it is DXVK, remove it; the server runs DXVK now.
[*]Copy the GTAIV folder from the zip over your GTAIV folder: d3d9.dll (client), dxvk.conf, and .trex\ with NvRemixBridge.exe (server), d3d9vk_x64.dll (DXVK 3.0.2) and bridge.conf.
[*]Keep dxvk.maxFrameRate and dxvk.latencySleep off in dxvk.conf; set the frame cap with clientFrameCap in .trex\bridge.conf. Run borderless.
[*]Launch normally. Logs: GTAIV\rtx-remix\logs\bridge32.log (client) and bridge64.log (server). If the game exits after about 12 seconds and bridge64.log was not written, launch again.
[/list]
Full text in INSTALL.md inside the zip.

[size=4][b]Reproducibility and future work[/b][/size]
The numbers above are one system's measurements, not a specification. Anyone reproducing them should report GPU, driver, resolution, the mean and 1% low over a fixed route, and the minimum largest free region over the session. Next: isolate the startup desync, validate on AMD and Intel drivers, reduce per-call client overhead, characterise multi-hour sessions.

[size=4][b]License and credits[/b][/size]
MIT, as upstream. NVIDIA bridge-remix (MIT) for the transport; DXVK by doitsujin (zlib), redistributed unmodified. The name is short for Broker Bridge, after the bridge in Liberty City.
